 multi-target actions support their classification as promising candidates for complex disorders that lack single-pathway etiologies. However, current evidence remains largely preclinical, with calls for large-scale, multicenter clinical trials to validate efficacy and safety in human populations.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"圆柚酮 interacts synergistically with 五味子木脂素 in neuroprotection\",\n            \"explanation\": \"圆柚酮 forms a key pharmacological partnership with 五味子木脂素, demonstrated by their combined use in mitigating cognitive dysfunction in Alzheimer’s mouse models. Their synergy involves activation of the PI3K/Akt/GSK-3β/mTOR pathway and suppression of amyloid-beta secretion and inflammatory proteins [Data: Relationships (28064)]. Additionally, this combination reduces NF-κB activity in cerebral ischemia-reperfusion injury models, underscoring a shared anti-inflammatory and neuroprotective mechanism. This interaction exemplifies a rational polypharmacy approach leveraging complementary molecular targets.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Immune checkpoint inhibitors are tentatively linked to Alzheimer’s disease treatment\",\n            \"explanation\": \"程序性死亡受体1单抗—a type of immune checkpoint inhibitor—is indicated for 阿尔茨海默病 in the context of gut microbiome-mediated therapeutic effects. While the primary evidence stems from studies showing that gut bacteria influence the bioactivation of neuroprotective compounds like curcumin, the mention of immune checkpoint inhibitors in this setting suggests exploratory research into immunomodulatory strategies for neurodegeneration [Data: Relationships (23852)]. However, this link is indirect and primarily associative, requiring further validation to establish causal therapeutic relevance.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Immune checkpoint inhibitors carry notable cardiovascular safety risks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Although not directly tied to